[
    {
        "content": "<p>When I was reading the <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Batteries/Data/List/Basic.html#List.inter\">documentation for <code>List.inter</code></a>, it says that it \"does not preserve multiplicity\", which sort of contradicts the example it gives: <code>[1, 1].inter [1]</code> equals <code>[1, 1]</code>, which seems to preserve multiplicity. The issue seems more complicated:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Batteries</span>\n\n<span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">eval</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">inter</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"c1\">-- [1, 1]</span>\n<span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">eval</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">inter</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"c1\">-- [1]</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>Hence, <code>List.inter</code> doesn't even seem like it's a commutative operation. Is the given description accurate? Should the example be changed to the latter one above?</p>",
        "id": 614623169,
        "sender_full_name": "Danny",
        "timestamp": 1785874633
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Note that failure of commutativity has nothing to do with multiplicity:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Batteries</span>\n\n<span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">eval</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"mi\">2</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">inter</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"mi\">2</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"c1\">-- [1,2]</span>\n<span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">eval</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"mi\">2</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">inter</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"mi\">2</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"c1\">-- [2,1]</span>\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 614626899,
        "sender_full_name": "Kevin Buzzard",
        "timestamp": 1785875994
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>That's a good point. I suppose I'm more surprised that swapping the order of the lists gives two different results that are not permutations of each other</p>",
        "id": 614627144,
        "sender_full_name": "Danny",
        "timestamp": 1785876078
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I guess I understand it a bit better: <code>List.inter</code> is explicitly meant to be about filtering the first list by the second and not about forming a list that contains the elements in common from both lists. I was approaching <code>List.inter</code> like a set intersection</p>",
        "id": 614628041,
        "sender_full_name": "Danny",
        "timestamp": 1785876407
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I mean, it kind of is about forming a set intersection: <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Batteries/Data/List/Lemmas.html#List.mem_inter_iff\">List.mem_inter_iff</a> It's just that, for sets with ordering and multiplicity, this doesn't fix it uniquely.</p>",
        "id": 614640934,
        "sender_full_name": "Alex Meiburg",
        "timestamp": 1785882975
    }
]